3	
4	\paragraph{Vision token efficiency.}
5	Token pruning in ViTs is well established: DynamicViT~\cite{rao2021dynamicvit}, EViT~\cite{liang2022evit}, and ToMe~\cite{bolya2023tome} consistently remove 30--60\% of tokens with $<$1\% accuracy loss on ImageNet. For VLMs, FastV~\cite{chen2024fastv}, LLaVA-PruMerge~\cite{shang2024prumerge}, and TokenPacker~\cite{li2024tokenpacker} extend pruning and spatial-aware compression to language-vision tasks. We test whether these results transfer to closed-loop VLA control, where action errors compound across timesteps.
6	
7	\paragraph{VLA-specific token reduction.}
8	VLA-Cache~\cite{xu2025vlacache} caches vision KV states across timesteps for 1.7$\times$ speedup without removing tokens. VLA-IAP~\cite{cheng2026vlaiap} uses interaction-aligned pruning on OpenVLA; EfficientVLA~\cite{yang2025efficientvla} combines layer pruning and token selection on CogACT~\cite{li2024cogact}; VLA-Pruner~\cite{liu2025vlapruner} uses dual-level importance criteria for temporal-aware pruning. All evaluate on MLP-projector architectures. None reports the cliff-edge failure we observe on pixel-shuffle VLAs; their positive results may not generalize to spatially entangled connectors.
9	
10	\paragraph{Vision connectors.}
11	Linear projections~\cite{black2024pi0,black2025pi05} apply no cross-token mixing, producing independently addressable tokens; $\pi_0.5$~\cite{black2025pi05} adds a learned $\times\!\sqrt{d}$ scaling that preserves near-uniform norms. Per-patch MLP projectors~\cite{liu2023llava,kim2024openvla} preserve per-token granularity but introduce partial importance differentiation through nonlinear transformations. Cross-attention connectors (Q-Former~\cite{li2023blip2}, Perceiver~\cite{alayrac2022flamingo,jaegle2021perceiver}, RoboFlamingo~\cite{li2024roboflamingo}) compress to learned queries at the architectural level. Pixel-shuffle connectors~\cite{shi2016subpixel,shukor2025smolvla,chen2024internvl} fold patches via channel concatenation, achieving training-time compression but creating inter-token dependencies; compact VLAs such as TinyVLA~\cite{wen2024tinyvla} adopt similar efficient connectors to reduce token counts. Our analysis links this taxonomy to inference-time compressibility: linear projections create uniform criticality where method selection matters more than retention ratio, MLP projectors permit graded pruning, and pixel-shuffle requires learned re-encoding.
12	
13	\paragraph{VLA token compression.}
14	Concurrent works compress tokens on MLP-projector VLAs: Compressor-VLA~\cite{gao2025compressorvla}, SpecPrune-VLA~\cite{wang2025specprunevla}, LightVLA~\cite{jiang2026lightvla}, and ``See What Matters''~\cite{feng2026seewhatmatters}. SparseVLM~\cite{zhang2025sparsevlm}, SparseVILA~\cite{khaki2025sparsevila}, and ``Don't Run with Scissors''~\cite{jabbour2025dontrun} explore sparsification and weight pruning fragility. All operate on MLP-projector architectures or evaluate without diagnosing connector-dependent failure modes. We identify why deletion catastrophically fails on pixel-shuffle architectures and validate re-encoding as a remedy.

