1	\section{Related Work}
2	\label{sec:related_work}
3	
4	\paragraph{Vision token efficiency.}
5	Token pruning in ViTs is well studied: methods select tokens by learned binary masks~\cite{rao2021dynamicvit}, attention-guided reorganization~\cite{liang2022evit}, bipartite matching~\cite{bolya2023tome}, or window-level activation sparsity~\cite{chen2023sparsevit}, consistently removing 30--60\% of tokens with $<$1\% accuracy loss on classification. Adaptive approaches vary computation per input: TokenLearner~\cite{ryoo2021tokenlearner} learns spatial attention maps for compact token selection, A-ViT~\cite{yin2022avit} applies per-token halting that terminates patches at different network depths, and AdaViT~\cite{meng2022adavit} jointly adapts patch, head, and block usage. For VLMs, pruning extends to the language backbone: FastV~\cite{chen2024fastv} removes tokens after early LLM layers, LLaVA-PruMerge~\cite{shang2024prumerge} and FasterVLM~\cite{zhang2024fastervlm} use CLS-token attention from the visual encoder as an importance signal, FitPrune~\cite{ye2025fitprune} minimizes attention-distribution divergence to produce layerwise pruning recipes without training, and CrossGET~\cite{shi2024crossget} applies cross-modal guided token merging. TokenPacker~\cite{li2024tokenpacker} takes a different approach with coarse-to-fine re-encoding of compressed representations. All evaluate on classification or VQA benchmarks; we test whether these results transfer to closed-loop VLA control, where action errors compound across timesteps.
6	
7	\paragraph{VLA-specific token reduction.}
8	VLA-Cache~\cite{xu2025vlacache} caches vision KV states across timesteps for 1.7$\times$ speedup without removing tokens. VLA-IAP~\cite{cheng2026vlaiap} uses interaction-aligned pruning on OpenVLA; EfficientVLA~\cite{yang2025efficientvla} combines layer pruning and token selection on CogACT~\cite{li2024cogact}; VLA-Pruner~\cite{liu2025vlapruner} uses dual-level importance criteria for temporal-aware pruning. All evaluate on MLP-projector architectures. None reports the cliff-edge failure we observe on pixel-shuffle VLAs; their positive results may not generalize to spatially entangled connectors.
9	
10	\paragraph{Vision connectors.}
11	Linear projections~\cite{black2024pi0,black2025pi05} apply no cross-token mixing, producing independently addressable tokens; $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{black2025pi05} adds a learned $\times\!\sqrt{d}$ scaling that preserves near-uniform norms. Per-patch MLP projectors~\cite{liu2023llava,liu2024llava15,kim2024openvla} preserve per-token granularity but introduce partial importance differentiation through nonlinear transformations. Cross-attention connectors (Q-Former~\cite{li2023blip2}, Perceiver~\cite{alayrac2022flamingo,jaegle2021perceiver}, RoboFlamingo~\cite{li2024roboflamingo}) compress to learned queries at the architectural level. Pixel-shuffle connectors~\cite{shi2016subpixel,shukor2025smolvla,chen2024internvl} fold $k{\times}k$ spatial patches into channel-concatenated tokens, achieving training-time compression but creating inter-token spatial dependencies. InternVL~\cite{chen2024internvl} and InternVL\,2.5~\cite{chen2024internvl25} apply pixel unshuffle with dynamic tiling to reduce tokens to one-quarter of the original count; Mini-InternVL~\cite{gao2024miniinternvl} distills this design into 1--4B parameter models; compact VLAs such as TinyVLA~\cite{wen2024tinyvla} adopt similar efficient connectors. Our analysis links this taxonomy to inference-time compressibility: linear projections create uniform criticality where method selection matters more than retention ratio, MLP projectors permit graded pruning, and pixel-shuffle requires learned re-encoding.
12	
13	\paragraph{VLA compression.}
14	Concurrent works compress tokens on MLP-projector VLAs: Compressor-VLA~\cite{gao2025compressorvla} uses instruction-guided compression with semantic and spatial modules, SpecPrune-VLA~\cite{wang2025specprunevla} combines self-speculative decoding with action-aware pruning, LightVLA~\cite{jiang2026lightvla} learns differentiable pruning masks during training, and ``See What Matters''~\cite{feng2026seewhatmatters} applies differentiable grid sampling for generalizable pruning. SparseVLM~\cite{zhang2025sparsevlm} and SparseVILA~\cite{khaki2025sparsevila} explore text-guided visual sparsification for VLM inference. ``Don't Run with Scissors''~\cite{jabbour2025dontrun} provides a complementary perspective: rather than token reduction, they study \emph{weight} pruning and quantization on VLA models, and find that standard magnitude- and gradient-based pruning causes drastic performance degradation and increased safety violations. Their proposed recovery method, GLUESTICK, interpolates between dense and pruned weight spaces without additional training. This finding parallels our observation that naive compression breaks VLA control, though the failure mechanisms differ: weight pruning degrades learned representations uniformly across the network, while vision token deletion selectively removes spatial information with consequences that depend on how the connector entangles tokens. All of the above works operate on MLP-projector architectures or evaluate without diagnosing connector-dependent failure modes. We identify why deletion catastrophically fails on pixel-shuffle architectures and validate re-encoding as a remedy.

